Leave out the TLS server_name extension when the name is an IP address.
RFC 6066 section 3 does not permit literal IPv4 or IPv6 addresses in
server_name, but the ClientHello carried whatever name it was given, so
"wget https://192.0.2.1/" and "ssl_client -n 192.0.2.1" sent the
address as SNI, which strict servers may reject with a fatal alert.
wget already had a TODO about this. Omit the extension when the name
contains a colon, which no host name does, or consists only of digits
and dots, which no host name does either since top-level domains are
not all-numeric.

---
 networking/tls.c |    7 ++++++-
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/networking/tls.c
+++ b/networking/tls.c
@@ -1619,8 +1619,13 @@
 	uint8_t *ptr;
 	int len;
 	int ext_len;
-	int sni_len = sni ? strnlen(sni, 127 - 5) : 0;
+	int sni_len;
 
+	/* RFC 6066 section 3: literal IPv4 and IPv6 addresses are not permitted */
+	if (sni && (strchr(sni, ':') || sni[strspn(sni, "0123456789.")] == '\0'))
+		sni = NULL;
+	sni_len = sni ? strnlen(sni, 127 - 5) : 0;
+
 	ext_len = 0;
 	ext_len += sizeof(extensions);
 	if (sni_len)
